    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Trio of New Morbidelli T802 Motorcycles Will Make Short Work of Every Surface\nPorsche Recalls Cayenne and 911 in China for Two Distinct Issues\n20 photos\nPhoto: Porsche\nOver in the People's Republic of China, plenty of Cayenne owners must pay a visit to their Porsche dealers of choice to address a rearview camera issue. Additionally, the German automaker has identified a single 911 with potentially damaged center-lock wheel fasteners.\nThe Chinese State Administration for Market Regulation published both notices, with the SAMR using S2026M0136I and S2026M0137V to identify these campaigns. Suspect Cayenne models were produced between November 22, 2017, and May 27, 2025, whereas the center-lock 911 was manufactured on August 22, 2024, in Germany.\nInternal combustion Cayennes hail from the Bratislava plant in Slovakia, which assembles the full-electric Cayenne line as well. As for the Neunelfer, the rear-engined icon is assembled exclusively in Zuffenhausen. Since 911 production began in 1964, the flagship sports car has been strictly tied to the German automaker's home base.\nRegarding the Cayenne 's problem, blame the parking assistance software. Because of iffy software, the rearview camera image may not appear on the central infotainment display. Without a rearview image, there's a higher risk of hitting an obstacle or another vehicle while reversing.\nGiven the root cause, Porsche dealers in China have been authorized to install remedy software. That's all, but unfortunately, the update must be performed via the OBD-II port. An estimated 18,274 such vehicles from the suspect timeframe have been sold in the Chinese market.\nPhoto: Porsche This campaign has a US counterpart in the form of NHTSA campaign 25V896 from December 2025, which covers a massive 173,538 vehicles. Its scope covers the Cayenne, Panamera, Taycan, and 911, with model years ranging from 2019 through 2025.\nAs for the 911 center-lock wheel fastener campaign, the owner of that model could experience a wheel detachment at high speed. The single-car safety recall expands two earlier centerlock wheel recalls from November and December 2024. The earliest campaign included 68 units of the 911 and 718, with production dates ranging from November 8, 2023, through September 4, 2024.\nThe newly added 911 falls within that original production timeframe. In a similar fashion to the Cayenne's rearview camera issue, this one isn't exclusively Chinese either. In the United States, the campaign originally covered 1,851 vehicles. More recently, Porsche Cars North America expanded it by 548 vehicles.\nThe October 2026 expansion includes certain 911 variants, along with the 718 Cayman GT4 RS and the 718 Boxster-derived Spyder RS. In the United States market, owners are urged not to drive their vehicles until dealers inspect them.\nAs of November 2026, centerlock wheels are offered on the Carrera S MT Package, the GTS line, the GT3 line, and the Turbo S. The Carrera S presently starts at $156,200 before destination, and the MT Package adds $10,900 to the coupe's price. For some reason or another, centerlock wheels cannot be had as a standalone option on the Carrera S for 2027.\nVia // SAMR\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nPorsche Cayenne Porsche 911 Porsche China recall\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1969 Chevy Corvette Spent 35 Years Parked Until Someone Bought It. Can He Bring It Back to Life?",
